--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Prior art this project builds on, and owes.

  cargo-acl (formerly cackle)     https://github.com/cackle-rs/cackle
    David Lattimore. MIT OR Apache-2.0.

    The nearest existing tool and a direct predecessor. It arrived at the same
    interception mechanism — RUSTC_WRAPPER, and replacing a compiled build script with a
    shell script — independently and years earlier, and it pairs that with static
    reachability analysis over compiled object code that buildclamp does not attempt at
    all. No buildclamp code is derived from it; the debt is to the design.

  @lavamoat/allow-scripts          https://github.com/LavaMoat/LavaMoat
    MetaMask. The npm ecosystem's answer to the same problem — dependency lifecycle scripts
    disabled by default, with an explicit allowlist — and the closest conceptual ancestor
    of this idea anywhere.

  birdcage                         https://github.com/phylum-dev/birdcage
    Phylum, GPL-3.0-or-later, archived 2026-07-06. Not used and not linked: its licence is
    incompatible with this one. Its shape — namespaces plus a syscall allowlist, with a
    capability enum rather than a permission bag — informed this design, and
    docs/adr/0003 records the four independent reasons it is not a dependency.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Written deliberately in-tree rather than taken from a dependency:

  the classic-BPF seccomp filter and its builder    crates/sandbox/src/linux/bpf.rs
  the seccomp user-notification supervisor          crates/sandbox/src/linux/seccomp.rs
  the namespace setup and the capability lock       crates/sandbox/src/linux/ns.rs
  per-proc-macro attribution                        crates/sandbox/src/linux/attribution.rs

The filter is a few dozen fixed instructions and it is the thing standing between cargo and
a third party's code. A reviewer who does not trust buildclamp has to be able to read it,
and a dependency that generated it would move the thing being audited out of the
repository. `seccompiler` and `libseccomp` are both good crates; that is the reason
neither is used here.

